# Remote control: laptop GUI, Raspberry Pi hardware

Run the RobStride GUI on a laptop while the motors are wired to a Raspberry Pi.

```bash
python main.py --remote usvz@rs04pi.local \
    --remote-path '~/Desktop/Robstride_Control_Gui/rpi_agent.py'
```

Nothing about using the GUI changes. Tabs, plots, presets, sweep, calibration,
Set ID and E-STOP all behave as they do on a directly attached adapter, because
the GUI cannot tell the difference.

---

## Why the split is where it is

The obvious design — a CAN-over-network bridge, where the Pi forwards raw frames
and the laptop keeps using its `Transport` interface — is a very small change and
it **does not work** on this stack.

The control loop is blocking request/reply, per motor, per tick. Six motors at
100 Hz is 600 round-trips a second, each needing its reply before the next goes
out: a budget of roughly **1.7 ms per round-trip**. WiFi round-trip time is
2-30 ms with spikes past 100 ms. The loop would collapse to a few Hz and every
motor would repeatedly trip its ~3 s `canTimeout`.

So the boundary sits higher up, between "the GUI decides a target" and "the loop
transmits it":

```
Laptop                          Raspberry Pi                  Motors
MainWindow  --- JSON/SSH --->   ControlWorker  --- CAN --->   can0..can4
  (UI rate)   20-50 Hz            100 Hz loop      1 Mbit/s
```

Network jitter now delays a setpoint instead of starving the loop. A 200 ms
hiccup is invisible; under the other design it is a fault.

## Why the GUI needed almost no changes

`MainWindow` touches the worker exactly two ways: it calls `post(Command)`, and
it connects to fourteen Qt signals. That is already a clean network boundary.

`RemoteWorker` declares the *same fourteen signals* and the *same* `post()`.
Only three small edits to `main_window.py` were needed:

1. `__init__` accepts a `remote` tuple.
2. `_start_worker` builds a `RemoteWorker` instead of a `ControlWorker`, and
   skips the `QThread` — `RemoteWorker.run()` owns a pipe, not a control loop,
   so it returns immediately and must not be driven by `QThread.started`.
3. The link starts **after** `_build_ui()`. `RemoteWorker.run()` can emit
   `log`/`error` synchronously if ssh fails at once, and those handlers write to
   widgets — starting it inside `_start_worker` reached a log view that did not
   exist yet. This was a real crash during bring-up.

