## Transport: why SSH stdio

The agent is launched as `ssh <host> 'python3 -u rpi_agent.py'` and spoken to
over the resulting pipe, one JSON object per line.

- No listening port, no firewall rules, no TLS to configure.
- Authentication and encryption come from SSH, which you already have.
- Bidirectional from the start: stdin carries commands, stdout carries telemetry.

**`python3 -u` is mandatory.** Without unbuffered output, Python holds telemetry
in a 4 KB buffer and the laptop sees nothing until the process exits.

Anything the agent prints to stdout that is not protocol JSON corrupts the
stream, so all its diagnostics go to **stderr**, which SSH forwards to the
laptop's terminal.

The agent is a plain program reading stdin — SSH is only the pipe. It can be
driven locally on the Pi for testing, which is a useful way to separate a link
fault from a motor fault:

```bash
printf '%s\n' \
  '{"cmd":"connect","kind":"socketcan","channel":"can4","bus_name":"can4","motors":[{"device_id":3,"model":"rs-03"}]}' \
  '{"cmd":"ping"}' | python3 -u rpi_agent.py
```

