## Protocol

**Laptop to Pi.** Commands serialise **generically** — class name plus dataclass
fields — so all twenty command types work with no per-command mapping, and any
command added later keeps working:

```json
{"cmd": "Command", "name": "SetTarget",
 "fields": {"device_id": 3, "position": 0.5, "velocity": null}}
```

Two need special handling:

- **`Connect`** carries a live `Transport` object, which cannot cross a pipe and
  refers to hardware on the wrong machine anyway — the laptop has no `can4`.
  Only the intent travels, as `{"kind": "socketcan", "channel": "can4"}`, and
  the Pi builds the real transport against its own hardware.
- **`SetLimits`** nests a `SafetyLimits` dataclass, flattened out and rebuilt in.

The agent also accepts a simpler verb protocol (`connect`, `enable`, `target`,
`mode`, `estop`, `ping`, `shutdown`) so a script or `publish.py` can drive it
without importing the driver stack.

**Pi to laptop.** One JSON object per line, each mapping to a Qt signal:

| `sig` | Becomes |
|---|---|
| `status` | `statusUpdated` (batched, one timestamp per group) |
| `power` | `powerUpdated` (batched) |
| `connection` | `connectionChanged` |
| `enabled` | `motorEnabledChanged` |
| `scan` / `inventory` / `collision` | the matching signal |
| `calibration` / `range` / `zero_state` | the matching signal |
| `motor_id` / `sweep_stopped` | the matching signal |
| `log` / `error` | `log` / `error` |

