## Setup

### On the laptop

```bash
ssh-keygen -t ed25519                    # if you do not have a key
ssh-copy-id usvz@rs04pi.local
ssh usvz@rs04pi.local 'echo ok'          # must print ok with no password
```

Push the code:

```bash
rsync -av --exclude '__pycache__' robstride_gui/ \
  usvz@rs04pi.local:~/Desktop/Robstride_Control_Gui/robstride_gui/
rsync -av rpi_agent.py usvz@rs04pi.local:~/Desktop/Robstride_Control_Gui/
```

`publish.py` stays on the laptop. `rpi_agent.py` goes to the Pi.

### On the Pi

```bash
sudo apt install -y python3-can python3-pyside6.qtcore can-utils
```

Bring up the CAN interfaces:

```bash
for i in 0 1 2 3 4; do
  sudo ip link set can$i down 2>/dev/null
  sudo ip link set can$i up type can bitrate 1000000
  sudo ip link set can$i txqueuelen 1000
done
ip -details link show can0
```

Three things matter here:

- **`down` first** — an interface already up refuses a bitrate change with
  `Device or resource busy`. This makes the loop safe to re-run.
- **1000000** is the RobStride bitrate. A mismatch gives no clear error:
  transmits appear to succeed, nothing replies, error counters climb.
- **`txqueuelen 1000`** — the CAN default is 10 frames, easy to overflow at
  100 Hz with several motors (`ENOBUFS`). Most guides omit this.

A healthy interface shows `UP` in its flags, `bitrate 1000000`, and
`state ERROR-ACTIVE`. **`ERROR-ACTIVE` is the good state** despite the name — it
means the controller is on the bus and participating. Worry about
`ERROR-PASSIVE` and `BUS-OFF`.

To survive a reboot, wrap those commands in a `oneshot` systemd unit. Use
`|| true` on each `ip link` call — without it, one missing interface aborts the
unit and the rest never come up.

### Run

```bash
python main.py --remote usvz@rs04pi.local \
    --remote-path '~/Desktop/Robstride_Control_Gui/rpi_agent.py'
```

The title bar reads `RobStride Control  -  remote: usvz@rs04pi.local`. Running
`python main.py` with no flags still uses a locally attached adapter.

