## Troubleshooting

**`ERROR: link silent 1.6s - motors stopped` right after connecting.** Fixed by
the startup-race change; make sure both `rpi_agent.py` and `remote_worker.py` are
the updated versions on their respective machines.

**`M3: E-STOP engaged - clear it before enabling`.** E-STOP latches. Click the
E-STOP button in the GUI to release it.

**`could not read position before enable` and `canTimeout write not acked`
together.** These two in combination mean **no motor is answering at that id**.

**`socketcan send failed: Failed to transmit: Network is down`.** The interface
exists but is down. python-can binds to a down interface without complaining and
does not bring it up — only `ip link` does. `SocketCANTransport` now checks
`IFF_UP` before binding and raises once with the command you need.

**`[timing] Inventory blocked the loop 2710 ms`.** The Scan range is too wide.
Discovery waits ~20 ms per empty id, so 0-127 costs ~2.5 s. Set the spinboxes
just above your highest CAN id — 0-5 takes about 0.15 s. Also disable motors
before pressing Detect: discovery runs on the control thread, and a long enough
stall exceeds the motors' `canTimeout`.

**Motor registered as the wrong model.** The Model dropdown defaults to `rs-04`.
Set it *before* pressing Add or Scan. The wrong model means wrong MIT scaling in
both directions.

**A joint moves in the sim but the motor does not.** Its binding was dropped for
having no connected motor. The GUI log now names them; check the motor has a tab
open and that its CAN bus is connected.

**`ModuleNotFoundError` on the Pi.** SSH runs a non-interactive shell that never
sources your venv, so the agent uses system Python. Either install the deps
system-wide, or point at the venv interpreter — the path string is passed to the
shell, so this works:

```
--remote-path '~/Desktop/venv/bin/python3 -u ~/Desktop/Robstride_Control_Gui/rpi_agent.py'
```

**`candump` shows nothing on an idle bus.** Expected. RobStride motors do not
broadcast — they only reply when addressed. Run `candump can4` on the Pi *while*
the GUI is connected and you will see command/reply pairs.

